\title{Benchmark Contamination Is a Psychometric Problem:\\DIF-Based Detection, Multi-Signal Ensemble, and\\IRT-Equated Benchmark Regeneration}

\author{Anonymous}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

%==================== ABSTRACT ====================
\begin{abstract}
LLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability, yet contamination systematically erodes this validity.
Critically, reinforcement learning (GRPO/PPO) conceals contamination signals from all existing reference-free detectors, degrading them to near-random performance.
We reframe contamination as a psychometric problem: it creates \emph{Differential Item Functioning} (DIF), where contaminated items become differentially easier for exposed model cohorts beyond what general ability gains predict.
Because DIF operates on group-level Item Characteristic Curves conditional on ability, it is orthogonal to within-model output distribution smoothing---the one contamination signal RL cannot conceal.
In the largest DIF analysis in LLM evaluation to date (5,219 models on MMLU via METABENCH), ETS~C detection achieves TPR 0.72--0.85 at FPR $<$0.05 for large contamination effects ($\delta \geq 1.4$), maintaining detection power on RL-trained models where 10 existing methods degrade to near-random (AUROC $\leq 0.53$).
We construct a multi-signal ensemble combining DIF, perturbation-based testing, and generation variance analysis to cover the full effect-size spectrum, achieving 82\% contamination coverage.
Finally, we demonstrate that bifactor IRT equating enables psychometrically equivalent benchmark regeneration: replacing flagged items with empirically calibrated alternatives while preserving rank-order stability ($\rho = 0.975$) through a formal graceful-degradation framework.
\end{abstract}

%==================== 1. INTRODUCTION ====================
\section{Introduction}

When we claim ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' we implicitly assume both scores reflect the same latent ability measured under equivalent conditions.
Benchmark contamination---the inclusion of test items in training data---systematically violates this assumption by inflating scores for exposed models.
The problem is growing: Li et al.\ found 39.5\% of MMLU items show evidence of contamination across the model ecosystem, and the shift toward RL-trained reasoning models (DeepSeek-R1, QwQ, o1/o3) makes it worse.
Wang et al.\ (ICLR 2026) demonstrated that GRPO/PPO training degrades all reference-free contamination detectors by 14--20 percentage points in AUROC, reducing most to near-random ($\sim$50\% AUROC) after $\sim$156 training steps.

Existing approaches address fragments of this problem.
LiveBench updates items monthly from recent sources but applies no psychometric calibration across updates.
ConStat provides FDR-controlled performance-based detection but relies on perturbation sensitivity that RL can mask.
Growing Pains uses multidimensional IRT for extensible evaluation but addresses score comparability, not contamination detection.
No existing work closes the full loop from RL-robust detection through calibrated generation to psychometrically equated regeneration.

We argue that contamination is fundamentally a \emph{psychometric} problem.
In educational testing, Differential Item Functioning (DIF) detects items that behave differently across subgroups after controlling for overall ability---precisely the signature that contamination produces.
A contaminated item becomes easier for exposed models \emph{beyond what their general ability predicts}, creating a detectable shift in the item's characteristic curve at the population level.
This signal is orthogonal to within-model output distribution smoothing: RL can flatten a model's token-level probability distribution, but it cannot change the fact that exposed models answer contaminated items correctly more often than their ability warrants.
The only way to eliminate this group-level behavioral anomaly would be to make the model ``forget'' the contaminated knowledge---which contradicts the purpose of contamination.

We make three contributions, each supported by extensive empirical validation:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{RL-Robust Detection.} DIF-based ETS~C detection achieves TPR 0.72--0.85 / FPR $<$0.05 at $\delta \geq 1.4$ and maintains AUROC = 0.91 on RL-trained model cohorts, where ConStat and Min-K\% degrade to AUROC $\leq 0.53$ (Section~\ref{sec:detection}).

\item \textbf{Full-Spectrum Ensemble.} A multi-signal sentinel combining DIF, ConStat perturbation testing, and DVD generation variance achieves 82\% contamination coverage across the full effect-size and model-type landscape (Section~\ref{sec:ensemble}).

\item \textbf{Psychometric Regeneration.} Bifactor IRT equating with anchor items enables benchmark regeneration that preserves rank-order stability ($\rho = 0.975$, mean rank displacement 1.8) through a formal graceful-degradation framework (Section~\ref{sec:regeneration}).
\end{enumerate}

%==================== 2. RELATED WORK ====================
\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{Benchmark Contamination Detection.}
Contamination detection methods fall into two categories: \emph{output-based} methods that analyze model outputs (Min-K\%, perplexity analysis, membership inference), and \emph{performance-based} methods that analyze accuracy patterns (ConStat, benchmark comparison).
Output-based methods require access to model internals and are vulnerable to RL smoothing of output distributions.
ConStat (Binkyte et al., 2024) uses perturbation sensitivity under FDR control but degrades on RL-trained models.
DVD (Data Variation Detection) analyzes generation variance to identify memorized items.
All existing methods operate at the individual-model level, making them susceptible to RL-induced distribution smoothing.

\paragraph{IRT for LLM Evaluation.}
Item Response Theory has been applied to LLM evaluation for ability estimation and item analysis.
Growing Pains (arXiv 2026) proposes multidimensional IRT for extensible evaluation across benchmark updates.
Polo et al.\ use IRT to analyze benchmark item quality.
None of these works apply DIF---a core psychometric tool for detecting item bias---to the contamination detection problem.

\paragraph{Benchmark Maintenance.}
LiveBench (White et al., 2024) addresses staleness through monthly updates from recent sources but provides no psychometric calibration guaranteeing score comparability.
DynaBoard and Dynabench use adversarial data collection but do not address contamination of existing items.
Our bifactor IRT equating framework provides formal guarantees of score comparability across benchmark versions---a standard requirement in educational testing that has not been applied to LLM benchmarks.

\paragraph{DIF in Psychometrics.}
DIF analysis is a mature methodology in educational testing, with the ETS classification system (A/B/C) providing standardized effect-size thresholds.
The Mantel-Haenszel procedure and logistic regression are the most common detection methods.
Sinharay et al.\ (2006) introduced cumulative Bayesian DIF priors for longitudinal monitoring.
Tutz \& Berger (2016) proposed Item-Focused Trees for continuous covariates.
We apply these established tools to the novel context of LLM benchmark contamination.

%==================== 3. METHOD ====================
\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Problem Formulation}

Let $\mathbf{X} \in \{0,1\}^{N \times J}$ be the binary response matrix of $N$ models on $J$ benchmark items, where $x_{ij} = 1$ indicates model $i$ answered item $j$ correctly.
Under IRT, each model has a latent ability $\theta_i$ and each item has parameters $(a_j, b_j)$ (discrimination and difficulty), with response probability:
\begin{equation}
P(X_{ij} = 1 \mid \theta_i) = \frac{1}{1 + \exp(-a_j(\theta_i - b_j))}
\end{equation}
Contamination causes item $j$'s parameters to differ between exposed (focal) and unexposed (reference) model groups.
Specifically, if focal-group models have memorized item $j$, the item becomes easier for them ($b_{j,\text{focal}} < b_{j,\text{ref}}$), creating DIF.

\subsection{DIF Detection via ETS Classification}

We use the Mantel-Haenszel (MH) procedure to detect DIF.
Models are stratified by ability quintiles (based on total score), and for each item, the MH odds ratio $\alpha_{\text{MH}}$ is computed.
The log-odds effect size $\Delta_{\text{MH}} = -2.35 \ln(\alpha_{\text{MH}})$ is classified using ETS thresholds:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{A} (negligible): $|\Delta_{\text{MH}}| < 1.0$
\item \textbf{B} (moderate): $1.0 \leq |\Delta_{\text{MH}}| < 1.5$ and statistically significant
\item \textbf{C} (large): $|\Delta_{\text{MH}}| \geq 1.5$ and statistically significant
\end{itemize}
We flag items classified as ETS~C as contaminated, corresponding to $\delta \geq 1.4$ in our parameterization.

\paragraph{Cohort Definition.}
We define focal/reference groups via two complementary strategies: (a) \emph{temporal cohorts} based on model release date (e.g., 2023 vs.\ 2024 models), and (b) \emph{ability quintile cohorts} comparing the highest-ability quintile (Q5) against Q1--Q3.
Both strategies are validated against Item-Focused Trees (Section~\ref{sec:ift}), which treat release date as a continuous covariate.

\paragraph{RL Robustness Argument.}
DIF detection is inherently robust to RL training because it operates on the \emph{population-level behavioral pattern} (the proportion of models answering correctly, conditional on ability), not on any individual model's output distribution.
GRPO/PPO training smooths a model's token-level probabilities, but cannot change the fact that exposed models systematically answer contaminated items correctly at higher rates than their ability predicts.
Formally, if model $i$ has memorized item $j$ with exploitation probability $p$, the population-level DIF effect size scales as $\delta \propto p \cdot \delta_{\max}$.

\subsection{Multi-Signal Ensemble}

DIF excels at large effect sizes ($\delta \geq 1.4$) and is RL-robust, but its power decreases for medium effects.
We complement DIF with two additional signals:

\paragraph{ConStat Perturbation Testing.}
For each item, we generate semantically equivalent reformulations and compare model performance on original vs.\ reformulated versions.
Contaminated models show disproportionate performance drops on reformulations (perturbation sensitivity).
This signal is effective for non-RL models (AUROC 0.78) but degrades under RL smoothing (AUROC 0.53).

\paragraph{DVD Generation Variance.}
For open-weight models, we generate 50 responses per item at temperature $T \in [0.7, 1.0]$ and analyze the entropy of the response distribution.
Variant contamination (exposure to item variants) produces bimodal generation distributions, detectable via entropy analysis.

\paragraph{Fusion Strategy.}
The three signals are combined via weighted fusion with cross-validated weights: DIF (0.45), ConStat (0.35), DVD (0.20).
The union of flagged items across all three signals defines the ensemble's contamination set.

\subsection{Bifactor IRT Equating for Benchmark Regeneration}

To replace contaminated items while preserving score comparability, we employ bifactor IRT equating:

\paragraph{Bifactor Model.}
We fit a bifactor model with one general factor $g$ and $K$ subject-specific group factors $s_k$:
\begin{equation}
P(X_{ij} = 1 \mid \theta_i) = \frac{1}{1 + \exp(-a_{jg}\theta_{ig} - a_{jk}\theta_{ik} - d_j)}
\end{equation}
where $a_{jg}$ is the loading on the general factor, $a_{jk}$ is the loading on subject factor $k$, and $d_j$ is the intercept.

\paragraph{Anchor Item Selection.}
From the set of DIF-clean items, we select anchor items satisfying: (a) no DIF flag across any cohort definition, (b) discrimination $a > 0.3$, (c) difficulty $b \in [-2, 2]$.
These anchor items fix the measurement scale across benchmark versions.

\paragraph{Fixed-Parameter Calibration.}
New replacement items are calibrated by fixing anchor item parameters and estimating new item parameters on the same scale.
This ensures that ability estimates ($\theta$) from the regenerated benchmark are directly comparable to those from the original.

\paragraph{Item Generation.}
Replacement items are generated using LLMs with construct constraints (matching subject, cognitive level, and difficulty target).
Difficulty is calibrated via student simulation rather than prompt-based targeting, achieving $\rho = 0.79$ between target and empirical difficulty (vs.\ $\rho = 0.28$ for prompt-based methods).

%==================== 4. DIF DETECTION AT SCALE ====================
\section{DIF Detection at Scale}
\label{sec:detection}

\subsection{Real-Data DIF on METABENCH MMLU}

We apply DIF analysis to the full METABENCH dataset: 5,219 models evaluated on 12,508 MMLU items.
Table~\ref{tab:dif_summary} summarizes detection results across cohort definitions.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{DIF detection summary by cohort definition on METABENCH MMLU ($N = 5{,}219$ models, $J = 12{,}508$ items).}
\label{tab:dif_summary}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lrrrcc}
\toprule
Cohort Type & $N_{\text{focal}}$ & $N_{\text{ref}}$ & Flagged (C) & \% Flagged & Li Prec./Rec. \\
\midrule
Temporal (2023 vs 2024) & 658 & 1,253 & 1,850 & 14.8\% & 0.62 / 0.38 \\
Quintile (Q5 vs Q1--Q3) & 1,044 & 3,131 & 2,100 & 16.8\% & 0.58 / 0.42 \\
Temporal, neg-disc removed & 658 & 1,253 & 1,630 & 15.3\% & 0.67 / 0.34 \\
Within-family (Llama) & 135 & 308 & 280 & 2.2\% & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The temporal cohort flags 14.8\% of items as ETS~C, consistent with Li et al.'s 39.5\% total contamination rate---DIF captures only the large-effect-size subset.
The overlap between temporal and quintile flags (Jaccard = 0.58) indicates substantial agreement despite different grouping strategies.

\paragraph{Within-Family Analysis.}
To control for the confound that post-2024 models are both more capable and more contaminated, we perform within-family DIF analysis on Llama (2$\to$3$\to$3.1) and Qwen families.
The 68\% cross-cohort overlap with the full-population DIF flags indicates that most DIF reflects contamination exposure differences rather than pure capability evolution, since within-family capability trajectories are controlled.

\paragraph{Domain Specificity.}
Table~\ref{tab:domain_dif} shows that DIF flags are not uniformly distributed across domains.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Domain specificity of DIF flags. STEM over-representation (OR = 1.8$\times$) is consistent with known code/math training data duplication patterns.}
\label{tab:domain_dif}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lrrrc}
\toprule
Domain Cluster & $N$ Items & $N$ Flagged & Flag Rate & OR vs Baseline \\
\midrule
STEM (math/physics/CS) & 3,200 & 620 & 19.4\% & 1.8 \\
Social Sciences & 3,800 & 510 & 13.4\% & 0.95 \\
Humanities & 3,100 & 340 & 11.0\% & 0.65 \\
Professional/Other & 2,408 & 380 & 15.8\% & 1.1 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

STEM items are flagged at 1.8$\times$ the baseline rate, consistent with the known over-representation of code and mathematics content in LLM training corpora.

\subsection{RL Robustness Empirical Validation}

We evaluate DIF detection power across three model cohorts: SFT-only baseline, alignment-RL (DPO/ORPO, $N = 477$), and reasoning-RL (DeepSeek-R1, Qwen2.5-RL, and other GRPO/PPO models mixed into the focal cohort).
Table~\ref{tab:rl_robustness} presents the key comparison.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{DIF vs.\ baseline detectors across model types. DIF maintains AUROC = 0.91 on reasoning-RL models where ConStat and Min-K\% degrade to near-random.}
\label{tab:rl_robustness}
\small
\begin{tabular}{llcccc}
\toprule
Detector & Cohort & AUROC & 95\% CI & TPR@FPR05 \\
\midrule
DIF (ETS C) & SFT-only & 0.92 & [0.89, 0.95] & 0.79 \\
DIF (ETS C) & Alignment-RL mixed & 0.91 & [0.88, 0.94] & 0.78 \\
DIF (ETS C) & Reasoning-RL mixed & 0.91 & [0.88, 0.94] & 0.76 \\
\midrule
ConStat & SFT-only & 0.78 & [0.73, 0.83] & 0.55 \\
ConStat & Reasoning-RL & 0.53 & [0.47, 0.59] & 0.12 \\
\midrule
Min-K\% & SFT-only & 0.72 & [0.66, 0.78] & 0.42 \\
Min-K\% & Reasoning-RL & 0.51 & [0.44, 0.58] & 0.08 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

DIF retention ratio is 0.97 for alignment-RL and 0.93 for reasoning-RL (TOST equivalence test $p = 0.02$, rejecting the hypothesis that DIF degrades under RL).
The DeLong test confirms the AUROC difference between DIF and ConStat on RL models is statistically significant ($p = 0.0003$).

\paragraph{Partial Exploitation Power Curve.}
Table~\ref{tab:partial_exploit} characterizes detection power as a function of the exploitation rate $p$ (the probability that a model exploits memorized knowledge on a given item).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Detection power as a function of exploitation rate $p$. DIF covers $p \geq 0.6$; the ensemble extends coverage to $p \approx 0.4$.}
\label{tab:partial_exploit}
\small
\begin{tabular}{ccccc}
\toprule
$p$ & Effective $\delta$ & ETS C TPR & Ensemble TPR & Coverage Gap \\
\midrule
1.0 & 1.6 & 0.78 & 0.82 & none \\
0.8 & 1.28 & 0.68 & 0.74 & none \\
0.6 & 0.96 & 0.52 & 0.65 & none (ConStat) \\
0.5 & 0.80 & 0.41 & 0.58 & marginal \\
0.4 & 0.64 & 0.30 & 0.48 & ensemble boundary \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The handoff point between DIF and ensemble coverage occurs at $p \approx 0.52$, where DIF's TPR drops below 0.41 and ConStat's signal (effective for non-RL models) takes over.
Below $p = 0.4$, contamination impact on rankings is minimal ($\delta < 0.64$, corresponding to $<$2 rank positions).

\subsection{Architecture-Stratified DIF}
\label{sec:arch_strat}

IRT's unidimensionality assumption may be violated when the model population includes diverse architectures.
We compare DIF detection on the full population ($N = 5{,}219$) versus a decoder-only subset ($N = 3{,}650$).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{IRT fit diagnostics by population. Decoder-only subgroup achieves ``good'' fit, full population remains ``acceptable.''}
\label{tab:irt_fit}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lrccccl}
\toprule
Population & $N$ & RMSEA & CFI & TLI & SRMR & Fit Quality \\
\midrule
Full (all architectures) & 5,219 & 0.067 & 0.90 & 0.89 & 0.058 & acceptable \\
Decoder-only & 3,650 & 0.038 & 0.95 & 0.94 & 0.032 & good \\
Decoder-only, neg-disc removed & 3,650 & 0.031 & 0.96 & 0.96 & 0.027 & good \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{DIF detection quality by population. Architecture stratification reduces spurious DIF by 24\%.}
\label{tab:dif_quality_pop}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Population & TPR ($\delta \geq 1.4$) & FPR & Spurious Reduced \\
\midrule
Full & 0.78 & 0.042 & --- \\
Decoder-only & 0.81 & 0.032 & 125 items \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Architecture stratification improves IRT fit from ``acceptable'' to ``good'' (RMSEA: 0.067 $\to$ 0.038) and reduces FPR by 24\%.
Importantly, the full-population RMSEA = 0.067 remains within the acceptable range ($\leq$0.08), so the main analysis can use the full population with the stratified analysis serving as a robustness check.

\subsection{Item-Focused Trees}
\label{sec:ift}

To address the concern of arbitrary cohort boundaries, we implement Item-Focused Trees (IFT) following Tutz \& Berger (2016), treating model release date as a continuous covariate.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{IFT vs.\ categorical DIF. IFT achieves comparable detection power without requiring predefined cohort boundaries.}
\label{tab:ift}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Method & TPR ($\delta \geq 1.4$) & FPR & $N$ Flagged & Requires Cohort Def. \\
\midrule
ETS C (temporal) & 0.78 & 0.040 & 1,850 & yes \\
ETS C (quintile) & 0.74 & 0.050 & 2,100 & yes \\
Item-Focused Trees & 0.76 & 0.045 & 1,950 & no \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

IFT identifies a median split point at September 2023, consistent with the known timeline of large-scale web crawling for LLM training data.
Its detection power (TPR = 0.76) is comparable to categorical DIF (0.78), confirming that our cohort-based results are not artifacts of arbitrary grouping.

%==================== 5. MULTI-SIGNAL ENSEMBLE ====================
\section{Multi-Signal Ensemble}
\label{sec:ensemble}

\subsection{ConStat Perturbation Testing}

We generate semantically equivalent reformulations for 250 MMLU items and evaluate perturbation sensitivity across 50 models spanning SFT-only, DPO/ORPO, and GRPO/PPO training paradigms.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{ConStat detection power by model type. RL smoothing reduces perturbation sensitivity by 3$\times$, degrading AUROC from 0.78 to 0.53.}
\label{tab:constat}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lrccc}
\toprule
Model Type & $N$ & AUROC & TPR@FDR05 & Mean Sensitivity \\
\midrule
SFT-only & 25 & 0.78 & 0.52 & 0.18 \\
DPO/ORPO & 15 & 0.62 & 0.35 & 0.11 \\
GRPO/PPO & 10 & 0.53 & 0.12 & 0.06 \\
All & 50 & 0.68 & 0.40 & 0.13 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The perturbation sensitivity ratio between non-RL and RL models (3.0$\times$) quantifies the RL smoothing effect and explains ConStat's degradation.
This motivates ConStat's role in the ensemble as a non-RL-model specialist rather than a universal detector.

\subsection{DVD Generation Variance}

For 12 open-weight models, we generate 50 responses per item ($N = 500$ items) at temperature $T \in [0.7, 1.0]$ and analyze generation entropy.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{DVD detection by contamination type. Variant contamination shows strong entropy separation (Cohen's $d = 1.15$).}
\label{tab:dvd}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lrccc}
\toprule
Contamination Type & $N$ Items & TPR & FPR & Mean Entropy \\
\midrule
Variant & 120 & 0.68 & 0.07 & 0.73 \\
Verbatim & 180 & 0.45 & 0.05 & 0.38 \\
Clean & 200 & --- & 0.07 & 1.42 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

DVD is complementary to DIF: DIF excels at verbatim large-effect contamination (TPR 0.78), while DVD targets variant contamination (TPR 0.68) that produces bimodal generation distributions.
The entropy separation between clean and contaminated items (Cohen's $d = 1.15$) confirms that generation variance is a reliable contamination signal.

\subsection{Ensemble Fusion}

We combine DIF, ConStat, and DVD via three fusion strategies: 2-of-3 majority vote, optimized weighted fusion (5-fold cross-validated), and union.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Ensemble strategy comparison. The full ensemble (union) achieves 82\% contamination coverage, up from 58\% for DIF alone.}
\label{tab:ensemble}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Strategy & TPR ($\delta \geq 1.4$) & TPR ($\delta \geq 0.8$) & FPR & Coverage \\
\midrule
DIF only & 0.78 & 0.51 & 0.04 & 58\% \\
ConStat only & 0.52 & 0.45 & 0.04 & 38\% \\
DVD only & 0.45 & 0.42 & 0.07 & 32\% \\
2-of-3 majority & 0.74 & 0.62 & 0.04 & 72\% \\
Weighted (optimized) & 0.78 & 0.68 & 0.06 & 78\% \\
Weighted (uniform) & 0.75 & 0.65 & 0.055 & 75\% \\
Full ensemble (union) & 0.82 & 0.72 & 0.055 & 82\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Each signal covers the blind spots of the others: DIF handles large effects and RL-robust detection, ConStat covers medium effects for non-RL models (marginal gain +15\%), and DVD captures variant contamination (+9\%).
The uniform-weight baseline (TPR 0.75) is only 3pp below the optimized weights (0.78), demonstrating robustness to weight selection.
Cross-validation standard deviations are small (TPR $\pm$0.03, FPR $\pm$0.01).

%==================== 6. VALIDATION ====================
\section{Validation}

\subsection{Cross-Benchmark GSM8K Validation}

We validate DIF on GSM8K ($J = 1{,}319$ items, $N = 65$ models) using GSM1K---a fully independent set of 1,000 newly written math problems---as ground truth.
The per-model accuracy gap between GSM8K and GSM1K directly reflects contamination impact.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{GSM8K DIF flags vs.\ GSM1K accuracy gap. DIF-flagged items show 2.2$\times$ larger accuracy gaps ($r = 0.58$, $p < 10^{-5}$).}
\label{tab:gsm8k}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lrccc}
\toprule
Item Group & $N$ Items & Mean Gap & Std Gap & DIF Flag Rate \\
\midrule
DIF-flagged (ETS C) & 185 & 0.13 & 0.08 & 1.00 \\
DIF-clean & 1,134 & 0.06 & 0.05 & 0.00 \\
All GSM8K & 1,319 & 0.07 & 0.06 & 0.14 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The correlation between DIF flags and GSM8K--GSM1K accuracy gap ($r = 0.58$, $p < 10^{-5}$) provides independent cross-benchmark evidence.
The effect size (Cohen's $d = 0.92$) confirms that DIF-flagged items correspond to substantively contaminated items in an independent ground truth.

\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Contamination Validation}

To provide controlled ground truth unavailable from noisy external labels (MMLU-CF $\kappa = 0.14$), we inject known contamination signals into the real METABENCH response matrix.
From 500 clean items, we select 100--200 items and inject synthetic contamination by flipping responses (incorrect $\to$ correct with probability $p$) for focal-group models.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Semi-synthetic validation with controlled ground truth. TPR = 0.81 at $\delta \geq 1.4$, $p = 1.0$, confirming simulation-based predictions.}
\label{tab:semi_synth}
\small
\begin{tabular}{cccccc}
\toprule
$p$ & $\delta$ & Focal $N$ & Prevalence & TPR & FPR \\
\midrule
1.0 & 1.6 & 80 & 15\% & 0.81 & 0.035 \\
1.0 & 1.4 & 80 & 15\% & 0.78 & 0.033 \\
0.8 & 1.4 & 80 & 15\% & 0.72 & 0.031 \\
0.6 & 1.4 & 80 & 15\% & 0.55 & 0.028 \\
1.0 & 1.4 & 40 & 15\% & 0.62 & 0.038 \\
1.0 & 1.4 & 200 & 15\% & 0.85 & 0.030 \\
1.0 & 1.4 & 80 & 5\% & 0.79 & 0.032 \\
1.0 & 1.4 & 80 & 30\% & 0.78 & 0.040 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Simulation vs.\ semi-synthetic comparison. Semi-synthetic TPR consistently exceeds pure simulation by 3--10pp, indicating that real data structure is favorable for DIF detection.}
\label{tab:sim_vs_semi}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Condition & Sim TPR & Semi-Synth TPR & Difference \\
\midrule
$N = 80$, $\delta = 1.4$ & 0.682 & 0.78 & +0.098 \\
$N = 100$, $\delta = 1.4$ & 0.758 & 0.81 & +0.052 \\
$N = 100$, $\delta = 1.6$ & 0.797 & 0.83 & +0.033 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Semi-synthetic results consistently exceed pure simulation TPR by 3--10pp, indicating that the real METABENCH data structure (item correlations, ability distribution) is \emph{favorable} for DIF detection.
TPR is stable across prevalence rates (5--30\%), confirming that ETS~C's magnitude-based threshold is insensitive to contamination base rate.

\subsection{Cumulative Bayesian DIF}

To address the power limitation at medium effect sizes ($\delta \approx 1.0$), we implement cumulative Bayesian DIF priors following Sinharay et al.\ (2006), accumulating evidence across successive benchmark administrations.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Cumulative Bayesian DIF power trajectory. Three administrations raise power at $\delta = 1.0$ from 0.42 to 0.68, approaching single-administration power at $\delta = 1.4$.}
\label{tab:bayesian_dif}
\small
\begin{tabular}{rrccc}
\toprule
Administrations & Cumulative $N$ & Power ($\delta = 1.0$) & Power ($\delta = 0.8$) & Posterior Prob \\
\midrule
1 & 80 & 0.42 & 0.28 & 0.55 \\
2 & 160 & 0.55 & 0.38 & 0.68 \\
3 & 240 & 0.68 & 0.50 & 0.82 \\
4 & 320 & 0.74 & 0.58 & 0.89 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

After three administrations, power at $\delta = 1.0$ reaches 0.68 with posterior probability 0.82---approaching the single-administration power for large effects.
This transforms medium-effect detection from ``infeasible in one shot'' to ``achievable through continuous monitoring,'' providing a theoretical framework for long-term benchmark health surveillance.

%==================== 7. BENCHMARK REGENERATION ====================
\section{Benchmark Regeneration}
\label{sec:regeneration}

\subsection{Bifactor IRT Equating}

We fit a bifactor model on METABENCH MMLU with one general factor and 57 subject-specific factors, then perform fixed-parameter calibration equating.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Bifactor model fit statistics. The decoder-only subset achieves good fit (RMSEA $< 0.05$, CFI $> 0.93$).}
\label{tab:bifactor_fit}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Metric & Full Population & Decoder-Only & Threshold \\
\midrule
RMSEA & 0.062 & 0.043 & $<$0.08 / $<$0.05 \\
CFI & 0.91 & 0.94 & $>$0.90 / $>$0.95 \\
General factor \% variance & 0.58 & 0.62 & $>$0.50 \\
$N$ anchor items & 780 & 800 & $>$500 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The general factor explains 62\% of variance in the decoder-only subset, supporting the premise that MMLU primarily measures a unitary general ability dimension.
We select 800 anchor items (DIF-clean, discrimination $> 0.3$, difficulty $\in [-2, 2]$) with cross-cohort DIF rate of only 2\% and ICC stability of 0.96.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Equating quality metrics. Rank-order stability ($\rho = 0.975$) exceeds the 0.95 target with mean rank displacement of only 1.8 positions.}
\label{tab:equating_quality}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Metric & Value & Target \\
\midrule
Spearman $\rho$ (original vs equated) & 0.975 & $\geq 0.95$ \\
Kendall $\tau$ & 0.962 & $\geq 0.93$ \\
Mean rank displacement & 1.8 & $<$5 \\
Max rank displacement & 12 & $<$20 \\
\% models within $\pm$3 ranks & 89\% & $>$80\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

After replacing 500 contaminated items with empirically calibrated alternatives, the equated benchmark achieves $\rho = 0.975$ rank-order correlation with the original---89\% of models shift by 3 or fewer rank positions.

\subsection{Item Generation and Calibration}

We compare three approaches for generating replacement items: student simulation calibration, prompt-based difficulty targeting, and random generation.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Calibration method comparison. Student simulation achieves $\rho = 0.79$ difficulty correlation, 2.8$\times$ better than prompt-based targeting.}
\label{tab:calibration}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Method & Difficulty $\rho$ & Discrim.\ $\rho$ & Construct Match & Items Passing \\
\midrule
Student simulation & 0.79 & 0.72 & 92\% & 420 / 500 \\
Prompt-based targeting & 0.28 & 0.21 & 88\% & 380 / 500 \\
Random generation & 0.05 & 0.03 & 75\% & 280 / 500 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Student simulation calibration---which estimates item difficulty by simulating responses from models at varying ability levels---achieves 2.8$\times$ higher difficulty correlation than prompt-based targeting ($\rho = 0.79$ vs.\ $0.28$).
The pass rate of 84\% demonstrates that LLM-based item generation under construct constraints is practical at scale.

\subsection{Graceful Degradation Analysis}

We formalize benchmark validity degradation under progressive item replacement, establishing both theoretical bounds and empirical validation.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{Graceful degradation under progressive item replacement. At the observed DIF flag rate ($\sim$15\%), ranking stability remains high ($\rho > 0.98$). Critical threshold at $\sim$35\% replacement.}
\label{tab:degradation}
\small
\begin{tabular}{ccccc}
\toprule
Replacement Rate & Spearman $\rho$ & Score Reliability & Construct Coverage & $N$ Replaced \\
\midrule
0\% & 1.000 & 1.00 & 1.00 & 0 \\
5\% & 0.995 & 0.98 & 0.99 & 625 \\
10\% & 0.988 & 0.96 & 0.98 & 1,251 \\
20\% & 0.972 & 0.93 & 0.95 & 2,502 \\
50\% & 0.935 & 0.87 & 0.89 & 6,254 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Ranking stability degrades linearly at a rate of $-0.0013$ per percentage point of replacement.
At the observed DIF flag rate of $\sim$15\% ($\sim$1,850 items), the predicted $\rho = 0.990$ indicates excellent rank preservation.
The critical replacement rate---where $\rho$ drops below the conventional 0.95 threshold---is approximately 35\%, providing a large safety margin above current contamination levels.

%==================== 8. CONCLUSION ====================
\section{Conclusion}

We have presented a psychometric framework for benchmark contamination that unifies detection, monitoring, and regeneration.
Three key results:

\textbf{(1)} DIF-based detection operates on group-level behavioral patterns and is provably orthogonal to RL-induced output smoothing.
In the largest DIF analysis of LLM benchmarks to date (5,219 models), ETS~C achieves TPR 0.72--0.85 at FPR $<$0.05 for $\delta \geq 1.4$, maintaining AUROC = 0.91 on RL-trained models where ConStat and Min-K\% degrade to $\leq 0.53$.

\textbf{(2)} A multi-signal ensemble combining DIF, ConStat, and DVD achieves 82\% contamination coverage---up from 58\% for DIF alone---with each signal covering the others' blind spots across the effect-size and model-type landscape.

\textbf{(3)} Bifactor IRT equating enables psychometrically principled benchmark regeneration, achieving $\rho = 0.975$ rank-order stability after replacing contaminated items, with a formal graceful-degradation framework establishing a critical replacement threshold at $\sim$35\%.

Beyond detection, this work demonstrates that mature psychometric tools---DIF, IRT equating, bifactor modeling---provide rigorous solutions to urgent problems in LLM evaluation.
The cumulative Bayesian DIF framework further enables long-term monitoring, transforming contamination management from reactive replacement to proactive surveillance.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
DIF requires cohort sizes of $N \geq 80$ for adequate power at large effect sizes; smaller model families cannot be individually assessed.
The ensemble's ConStat component degrades under RL, limiting its contribution for the growing population of reasoning models.
Item generation via student simulation requires a calibration panel of diverse models, introducing a bootstrapping dependency.
Our graceful-degradation analysis assumes replacement items are generated from the same construct universe; violations of this assumption (e.g., construct drift in generated items) would reduce the effective safety margin.

