\end{table}

Architecture stratification improves IRT fit from ``acceptable'' to ``good'' (RMSEA: 0.067 $\to$ 0.038) and reduces FPR by 24\%.
Importantly, the full-population RMSEA = 0.067 remains within the acceptable range ($\leq$0.08), so the main analysis can use the full population with the stratified analysis serving as a robustness check.

\subsection{Item-Focused Trees}
\label{sec:ift}

To address the concern of arbitrary cohort boundaries, we implement Item-Focused Trees (IFT) following Tutz \& Berger (2016), treating model release date as a continuous covariate.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{IFT vs.\ categorical DIF. IFT achieves comparable detection power without requiring predefined cohort boundaries.}
\label{tab:ift}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Method & TPR ($\delta \geq 1.4$) & FPR & $N$ Flagged & Requires Cohort Def. \\
\midrule
ETS C (temporal) & 0.78 & 0.040 & 1,850 & yes \\
ETS C (quintile) & 0.74 & 0.050 & 2,100 & yes \\
Item-Focused Trees & 0.76 & 0.045 & 1,950 & no \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

IFT identifies a median split point at September 2023, consistent with the known timeline of large-scale web crawling for LLM training data.
Its detection power (TPR = 0.76) is comparable to categorical DIF (0.78), confirming that our cohort-based results are not artifacts of arbitrary grouping.

%==================== 5. MULTI-SIGNAL ENSEMBLE ====================
\section{Multi-Signal Ensemble}
\label{sec:ensemble}

\subsection{ConStat Perturbation Testing}

We generate semantically equivalent reformulations for 250 MMLU items and evaluate perturbation sensitivity across 50 models spanning SFT-only, DPO/ORPO, and GRPO/PPO training paradigms.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\caption{ConStat detection power by model type. RL smoothing reduces perturbation sensitivity by 3$\times$, degrading AUROC from 0.78 to 0.53.}
\label{tab:constat}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lrccc}
\toprule
Model Type & $N$ & AUROC & TPR@FDR05 & Mean Sensitivity \\
\midrule
SFT-only & 25 & 0.78 & 0.52 & 0.18 \\
