%==================== 8. CONCLUSION ====================
\section{Conclusion}

We have presented a psychometric framework for benchmark contamination that unifies detection, monitoring, and regeneration.
Three key results:

\textbf{(1)} DIF-based detection operates on group-level behavioral patterns and is provably orthogonal to RL-induced output smoothing.
In the largest DIF analysis of LLM benchmarks to date (5,219 models), ETS~C achieves TPR 0.72--0.85 at FPR $<$0.05 for $\delta \geq 1.4$, maintaining AUROC = 0.91 on RL-trained models where ConStat and Min-K\% degrade to $\leq 0.53$.

\textbf{(2)} A multi-signal ensemble combining DIF, ConStat, and DVD achieves 82\% contamination coverage---up from 58\% for DIF alone---with each signal covering the others' blind spots across the effect-size and model-type landscape.

\textbf{(3)} Bifactor IRT equating enables psychometrically principled benchmark regeneration, achieving $\rho = 0.975$ rank-order stability after replacing contaminated items, with a formal graceful-degradation framework establishing a critical replacement threshold at $\sim$35\%.

Beyond detection, this work demonstrates that mature psychometric tools---DIF, IRT equating, bifactor modeling---provide rigorous solutions to urgent problems in LLM evaluation.
The cumulative Bayesian DIF framework further enables long-term monitoring, transforming contamination management from reactive replacement to proactive surveillance.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
DIF requires cohort sizes of $N \geq 80$ for adequate power at large effect sizes; smaller model families cannot be individually assessed.
The ensemble's ConStat component degrades under RL, limiting its contribution for the growing population of reasoning models.
Item generation via student simulation requires a calibration panel of diverse models, introducing a bootstrapping dependency.
Our graceful-degradation analysis assumes replacement items are generated from the same construct universe; violations of this assumption (e.g., construct drift in generated items) would reduce the effective safety margin.

